  {
    q: "What is EST?",
    a: "EST stands for Eastern Standard Time. It is the winter time zone used on the East Coast of North America (including New York, Toronto, Miami, and Washington D.C.), operating at UTC-5 (5 hours behind Coordinated Universal Time)."
  },
  {
    q: "What is the exact time difference between AKDT and EST?",
    a: "Eastern Standard Time (EST) is exactly 3 hours ahead of Alaska Daylight Time (AKDT). For example, when it is 9:00 AM AKDT in Anchorage, it is 12:00 PM (Noon) EST in New York."
  },
  {
    q: "When is AKDT active during the year?",
    a: "In Alaska, AKDT begins on the second Sunday of March (when clocks jump forward 1 hour from AKST) and ends on the first Sunday of November (when clocks fall back to AKST)."
  },
  {
    q: "Which Alaskan cities observe Alaska Daylight Time (AKDT)?",
    a: "Major cities observing AKDT include Anchorage, Fairbanks, Juneau, Sitka, Ketchikan, Wasilla, Kenai, and Kodiak."
  },
  {
    q: "Which North American cities observe Eastern Standard Time (EST)?",
    a: "Major cities observing EST in winter include New York City, Toronto, Montreal, Boston, Philadelphia, Washington D.C., Atlanta, and Miami."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I convert AKDT to EST manually?",
    a: "To convert AKDT to EST, add 3 hours to the AKDT timestamp. For example: 8:00 AM AKDT + 3 hours = 11:00 AM EST. If the addition exceeds 24:00, move to the next day."
