<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="content" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Pitfalls in AKDT to EST Conversions</h2>
  <article>
    <h3>1. Confusing Alaska Standard Time (AKST) with AKDT</h3>
    <p>
      AKST is UTC-9 (winter), whereas AKDT is UTC-8 (summer). Using winter offsets during summer months introduces a 1-hour scheduling error.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>2. Ignoring Western Aleutian Island Differences</h3>
    <p>
      The western Aleutian Islands (Adak) operate on Hawaii-Aleutian Time (HDT, UTC-9), which is 1 hour behind Anchorage (AKDT).
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>3. Conflating EST with EDT</h3>
    <p>
      EST is UTC-5 (winter), while EDT is UTC-4 (summer). When both regions observe summer daylight saving (AKDT and EDT), the gap is 4 hours.
    </p>
  </article>
  <article>
    <h3>4. Date Boundary Errors on Midnight Flight Logs</h3>
    <p>
      Converting late-night AKDT timestamps (such as 10:00 PM AKDT) yields 1:00 AM EST on the following day. Always confirm date tags on flight manifests.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- TECHNICAL TIMEZONE DATABASE SECTION -->
<section class="content" id="technical-details" aria-labelledby="tech-heading">
  <h2 id="tech-heading">Technical Specifications & IANA Timezone Database Metadata</h2>
  <p>
    Software developers require exact database keys to handle automated timezone calculations across enterprise systems.
  </p>
  <div class="method-grid">
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>Canonical Identifiers</h3>
      <p>The primary IANA time zone key for Alaska is <code>America/Anchorage</code>. Additional Alaskan aliases include <code>America/Juneau</code> and <code>America/Sitka</code>.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>ISO 8601 Offset Format</h3>
      <p>AKDT is formatted in ISO 8601 strings as <code>-08:00</code> (e.g., <code>2026-07-30T10:00:00-08:00</code>).</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>POSIX Timezone Specification</h3>
      <p>The standard POSIX string for Alaska Time is <code>AKST9AKDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0</code>, defining March and November daylight transitions.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="method-card">
      <h3>FAA Air Traffic Control Synchronization</h3>
      <p>Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) control towers across Alaska log all flight data in UTC baseline and translate to local AKDT/EST for regional flight centers.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>

